      "pao" : {
        "person" : "Walt Whitman",
        "action" : "breathing the river air and gazing across at Manhattan",
        "object" : "the railing cut with his lines",
        "notes" : "“So long as men can breathe or eyes can see,” Walt Whitman leans on the railing of Fulton Ferry Landing, where lines of his “Crossing Brooklyn Ferry” are cut into the metal, filling his lungs with river air and gazing across at Manhattan, as he wrote that every generation after him would."
